Since you’re interested in Santa work, I thought I’d send you Heather’s guide to seasonal jobs. The nice thing about seasonal work is that there are opportunities almost all year if you’re willing to switch from one season to the next.

January–March

• Tax preparation assistant
• Valentine’s Day gift shops, florists, and candy stores
• Winter resorts and ski areas
• Convention and trade show staffing

April–May

• Easter events and Easter Bunny photos
• Garden centers and nurseries
• Baseball stadiums
• Theme parks beginning their summer hiring
• National parks and campgrounds

June–August

• Summer camps
• Water parks
• County fairs
• Concert venues
• Tourism and sightseeing attractions
• Hotel and resort positions
• Lifeguarding and recreation programs

September–October

• Halloween stores
• Haunted houses
• Pumpkin patches
• Corn mazes
• Oktoberfest events
• Harvest festivals

November–December

• Santa Claus
• Mrs. Claus
• Santa’s elves
• Holiday photo staff
• Retail holiday hiring
• Gift wrapping
• Christmas tree lots
• Holiday light displays
• Package delivery helpers
• Seasonal warehouse work

Year-round possibilities

• Sports stadiums and arenas
• Convention centers
• Concerts and festivals
• Museums and zoos
• Catering and banquet events
• Election worker (during election years)
• Census work (during census years)

It’s an interesting way to think about employment. Instead of looking for one permanent job, you build a calendar of recurring seasonal opportunities and move from one to the next throughout the year.

—Heather
